    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/sqlite-connect/", "sqlite_connect", "API Functions", "sqlite_connect\nUse `sqlite_connect` to open an SQLite database. Call `sqlite_disconnect` when you finish with the handle.\n\nSQLite handle, or zero when the connection fails.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/sqlite-disconnect/", "sqlite_disconnect", "API Functions", "sqlite_disconnect\nUse `sqlite_disconnect` to close an SQLite connection. Do not use the handle after this call.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/sqlite-info/", "sqlite_info", "API Functions", "sqlite_info\n`sqlite_info()` reads the latest error text, the last inserted row ID, or the changed-row count.\n\nUse a converter with a fallback because the values have different types.\n\nhandle : the SQLite connection handle\nkey : `error`, `insert_id`, or `affected_rows`\nThe value for `key`.\nThe function stops with an error for an unknown key. The error lists the valid keys.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/sqlite-query/", "sqlite_query", "API Functions", "sqlite_query\nUse `sqlite_query` to run one SQL statement. Use `:name` placeholders with a parameter map.\n\nQuery result as `dval`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/str-ends-with/", "str_ends_with", "API Functions", "str_ends_with\nUse `str_ends_with` to test a string suffix.\n\n`true` when the text ends with the suffix.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/str-starts-with/", "str_starts_with", "API Functions", "str_starts_with\nUse `str_starts_with` to test a string prefix.\n\n`true` when the text starts with the prefix.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/string/", "string", "API Functions", "string\n`string()` reads a scalar as text. Request values are `dval`, so this is the normal way to read request fields.\n\n`value` accepts scalar values through `as string`. The conversion keeps source text exact. Text returns unchanged. Numbers use exact round-trip text. Booleans become `true` or `false`.\n\n`opt.fallback` is the result for empty text or a value with no text form. It defaults to the empty string. Empty and missing `dval` values both convert to empty text. Use the `?` suffix before conversion when presence matters. `value?` is false for a missing value and true for an empty value.\n\nThe options map accepts only `fallback`. An unknown key, including `base`, is an error.\n\n## The other converters\n\n`s64()`, `u64()`, `s32()`, and `f64()` use the same `value, opt` shape. Their options also support `base` for integer conversion. `bool()` keeps a `dval` first parameter, because strings cannot be conditions.\n\nvalue : scalar value or text to convert\nopt : options map with `fallback`\nThe converted text, or `opt.fallback`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/strpos/", "strpos", "API Functions", "strpos\nUse `strpos` to find text in a string. The result uses a zero-based position.\n\nFirst match position, or `-1`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/substr/", "substr", "API Functions", "substr\nUse `substr` to get part of a string. A negative start or length counts from the end.\n\nExtracted text.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/task/", "task", "API Functions", "task\nUse `task` to submit a task handler. Pass a unit path and a `dval` value for its input.\n\nTask identifier.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/task-await/", "task_await", "API Functions", "task_await\nUse `task_await` to wait for a task status. It returns when the task ends or the timeout expires.\n\nTask status map.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/task-cancel/", "task_cancel", "API Functions", "task_cancel\nUse `task_cancel` to request task cancellation. Check the returned status because a task can finish first.\n\nTask status map.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/task-status/", "task_status", "API Functions", "task_status\nUse `task_status` to read a task status.\n\nTask status map.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time/", "time", "API Functions", "time\n`time` returns the current Unix timestamp with second precision.\n\ncurrent Unix timestamp with second precision") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time-format-local/", "time_format_local", "API Functions", "time_format_local\n`time_format_local` formats a Unix timestamp in the server local time zone.\n\nformat : date format\ntimestamp : Unix timestamp. Zero uses the current time\nformatted local time") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time-format-relative/", "time_format_relative", "API Functions", "time_format_relative\n`time_format_relative` formats a timestamp relative to the current time.\n\ntimestamp : Unix timestamp\nvery_recent : text for very recent timestamps\nmedium_seconds : very recent cutoff in seconds\nmedium_recent : text after the very recent cutoff\nnot_recent_seconds : medium recent cutoff in seconds\nnot_recent : text after the medium recent cutoff\nformatted relative time") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time-format-utc/", "time_format_utc", "API Functions", "time_format_utc\n`time_format_utc` formats a Unix timestamp in UTC.\n\nformat : date format\ntimestamp : Unix timestamp. Zero uses the current time\nformatted UTC time") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time-parse/", "time_parse", "API Functions", "time_parse\n`time_parse` converts date or time text to a Unix timestamp.\n\nvalue : date or time text\nparsed Unix timestamp, or zero when parsing fails") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/time-precise/", "time_precise", "API Functions", "time_precise\n`time_precise` returns the current Unix timestamp with fractional seconds.\n\ncurrent Unix timestamp with fractional seconds") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/trap/", "trap", "API Functions", "trap\n`trap` stops the current handler.\n\nno value because execution stops\n`trap` always aborts execution.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/trim/", "trim", "API Functions", "trim\n`trim` removes leading and trailing whitespace.\n\nraw : text to trim\ntext without leading or trailing whitespace") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/u64/", "u64", "API Functions", "u64\n`u64()` converts the exact text from `value`. It uses base 10 unless `opt.base` selects another integer base. `opt.fallback` is the result for empty text or text that does not parse. It defaults to `0`.\n\n`value` accepts scalar values through `as string`. The conversion keeps scalar text exact. Empty and missing `dval` values both convert to empty text. Use `value?` before conversion when presence matters. An empty value is present, but a missing value is not.\n\n`u64(dval(true))` now returns the fallback. `true` converts to the text `\"true\"`, which is not a number.\n\nThe options map accepts only `fallback` and `base`. An unknown key is an error.\n\nvalue : scalar value or text to convert\nopt : options map with `fallback` and `base`\nA converted `u64`, or `opt.fallback`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unique/", "unique", "API Functions", "Get unique strings\n`unique` keeps the first copy of each string in a list-shaped `dval`.\n\nThe result keeps input order. This function does not change `items`.\n\nThis function returns a new list-shaped `dval`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unit-call/", "unit_call", "API Functions", "unit_call\n`unit_call` calls an exported function in a unit.\n\nfile_name : unit path\nfunction_name : exported function name\nprops : function input\nexported function result") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unit-compile/", "unit_compile", "API Functions", "unit_compile\n`unit_compile` compiles a unit.\n\npath : unit path. An empty path selects the current unit\n`true` if the compiler compiles the unit") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unit-info/", "unit_info", "API Functions", "unit_info\n`unit_info` returns metadata for a unit.\n\npath : unit path. An empty path selects the current unit\nunit metadata") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unit-load/", "unit_load", "API Functions", "unit_load\n`unit_load()` loads a unit and returns a handle you can call exported functions on. It is the way one unit calls another unit's code, as opposed to `component()`, which renders another unit's *output*.\n\nThe target unit must publish the names it offers with `#exports`. Anything not exported is invisible to callers.\n\n## Calling through the handle\n\nTwo spellings do the same thing. Member syntax is the readable one:\n\n    var target := unit_load(\"/tests/capy-module-target.capy\")\n    var result := target.echo({name: \"Ada\"})\n\n`call()` is the explicit form, and the one to use when the function name is itself a value:\n\n    var result := call(target, \"echo\", {name: \"Ada\"})\n\nEvery exported function takes at most one `dval` input and returns a `dval`. Calling with no argument passes an empty `dval`.\n\n## When to load a handle, and when not to\n\nLoad a handle once and reuse it when several calls hit the same unit — the handle carries the resolved, compiled unit, so repeat calls skip the lookup.\n\nFor a single call, `unit_call(path, name, input)` does the same work in one step without a handle. Prefer it for one-offs.\n\n## What a handle is\n\nA `module` value is opaque: there is nothing inside it to read, print, or compare. It is request-local — a handle is valid for the request that created it and cannot be stored and reused later. It copies across `dval` boundaries, so passing one around within a request is safe.\n\nValues crossing a call are copied, not shared. Mutating the `dval` you passed in does not affect what the callee saw, and mutating the result does not reach back into the callee.\n\n## Related entry points\n\n`unit_render()` runs a unit's `RENDER` handler and writes its output to the response. `unit_info()` reports a unit's exports and compile state; `unit_compile()` compiles a unit without calling it.\n\npath : the unit path to load\nA `module` handle for the unit.\nA path that does not resolve, a unit that fails to compile, or a name the target does not export raises a runtime error that aborts the request. Use `unit_info()` first when a unit's availability is genuinely optional.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/unit-render/", "unit_render", "API Functions", "unit_render\n`unit_render` runs a unit `RENDER` handler.\n\nname : unit path") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/units-list/", "units_list", "API Functions", "units_list\n`units_list` returns known unit paths.\n\na `dval` list of unit paths") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/upper/", "upper", "API Functions", "upper\n`upper` changes lowercase ASCII characters to uppercase.\n\nvalue : string to convert\nstring with lowercase ASCII characters changed to uppercase") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/uri-decode/", "uri_decode", "API Functions", "uri_decode\n`uri_decode` decodes a URI-encoded string.\n\nvalue : URI-encoded string\ndecoded string") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/uri-encode/", "uri_encode", "API Functions", "uri_encode\n`uri_encode` encodes a string for a URI component.\n\nvalue : string to encode\nURI-encoded string") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/usleep/", "usleep", "API Functions", "usleep\n`usleep` pauses the current handler for `usec` microseconds.\n\nusec : microseconds to sleep\n`0` after the sleep") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/values/", "values", "API Functions", "Get child values\n`values(value)` returns a new list of child values. A scalar returns a one-item list.\n\nvalue : source value\nA list of copied child values.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/var-dump/", "var_dump", "API Functions", "var_dump\n`var_dump` formats a `dval` as a string.\n\nvalue : value to format\nprefix : text before the formatted value\npostfix : text after the formatted value\nformatted string") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/ws-close/", "ws_close", "API Functions", "ws_close\n`ws_close()` closes the current WebSocket client. Pass a client ID to close that client.\n\nconnection_id : client ID. The default is the current client.\n`true` when the server queues the close.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/ws-send/", "ws_send", "API Functions", "ws_send\n`ws_send()` sends a message to every client in the current scope. Pass `scope` to select a scope.\n\nmessage : message bytes to send\nbinary : `true` for a binary frame\nscope : scope identifier\n`true` when the server queues the message for a client.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/ws-send-to/", "ws_send_to", "API Functions", "ws_send_to\n`ws_send_to()` sends a message to one client.\n\nconnection_id : client ID\nmessage : message bytes to send\nbinary : `true` for a binary frame\n`true` when the server queues the message.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/xml-decode/", "xml_decode", "API Functions", "xml_decode\n`xml_decode()` decodes XML source into a `dval`.\n\nsource : XML source\nA `dval` that contains the decoded XML.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/xml-encode/", "xml_encode", "API Functions", "xml_encode\n`xml_encode()` encodes a `dval` as XML. The default root name is `root`.\n\nvalue : value to encode\nroot_name : root element name\nThe XML source.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/yaml-decode/", "yaml_decode", "API Functions", "yaml_decode\n`yaml_decode()` decodes YAML source into a `dval`.\n\nsource : YAML source\nA `dval` that contains the decoded YAML.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/yaml-encode/", "yaml_encode", "API Functions", "yaml_encode\n`yaml_encode()` encodes a `dval` as YAML.\n\nvalue : value to encode\nThe YAML source.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/zip-create/", "zip_create", "API Functions", "zip_create\n`zip_create()` creates a ZIP archive from a `dval` map of entry names and content.\n\npath : archive path\nentries : archive entries\n`true` when the server creates the archive.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/zip-extract/", "zip_extract", "API Functions", "zip_extract\n`zip_extract()` extracts every entry from a ZIP archive.\n\npath : archive path\ndestination : destination directory\n`true` when the server extracts the archive.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/zip-list/", "zip_list", "API Functions", "zip_list\n`zip_list()` returns ZIP archive metadata and entries.\n\npath : archive path\nA `dval` with archive metadata and entries.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/api/zip-read/", "zip_read", "API Functions", "zip_read\n`zip_read()` returns one ZIP archive entry.\n\npath : archive path\nentry : entry name\nThe uncompressed entry bytes.") { found = true }
    print("</section>")
    print("<section class=\"search-section search-type\"><h3>Types</h3>")
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/type/dvalue/", "dvalue", "Types", "Dynamic values\nA `dval` stores scalar values, maps, lists, current-workspace callables, and `none`.\n\nUse maps for named fields and lists for ordered values. Read children with `value.name`, `value[\"name\"]`, or `value[0]`. A missing read returns `none` and does not change the value.\n\nUse `has` to test a key. Use `get` when absence is valid. Use `string`, `bool`, `s32`, `s64`, `u64`, or `f64` to convert a value.\n\nUpdate functions such as `set`, `push`, and `clear` change their target and return it. `keys` and `values` return new values.\n\nA callable read requires its exact function type. A mismatch traps. Public BRRB, JSON, YAML, and XML replace a callable with `none`. Maps keep keys. Lists keep indexes.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/type/request/", "request", "Types", "Request snapshot\nEvery Capy handler receives one parameter, `request`, of type `dval`. It is a snapshot of the request taken when the handler is entered — reading it never touches the network, and reading the same field twice always gives the same answer.\n\nRead scalars through a converter with a fallback, so a key that is absent has a defined value rather than an error:\n\n    var name := string(request.query.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n    var page := s64(request.query.page, {fallback: 1})\n    var debug := bool(request.query.debug)\n\nTest for presence with the `?` suffix, which is `true` when the key exists:\n\n    if request.query.token? { print(\"token supplied\\n\") }\n\n## Fields\n\n| Field | Type | Contains |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `method` | string | `GET`, `POST`, and so on |\n| `query` | map | parsed query-string parameters |\n| `form` | map | parsed form body parameters |\n| `params` | map | merged query and form parameters. Form values win when keys match. |\n| `body` | string | the raw request body |\n| `headers` | map | request headers, lowercased with dashes: `content-type`, `x-request-id` |\n| `cookies` | map | request cookies by name |\n| `files` | array | uploaded files, each with `name`, `size`, `temporary_path` |\n| `route` | map | `path`, `page`, `raw_path`, `valid` |\n| `url` | map | `script`, `base` |\n| `server` | map | remaining server and CGI variables, original uppercase names |\n| `session` | map | the current session snapshot |\n| `props` | map | properties passed by a component caller |\n| `config` | map | request-visible configuration |\n| `connection` | map | connection information |\n| `unit` | string | the source path of the unit currently executing |\n| `websocket` | map | `connection_id`, `scope`, `opcode`, `binary`, `connections` |\n| `call` | map | dispatch information for the current call |\n| `out` | map | mutable response state: `status`, `header`, `cookie`, and `buffer` |\n\n## Headers and server variables\n\n`headers` holds anything that arrived as an HTTP header, renamed for readability: the transport's `HTTP_X_REQUEST_ID` appears as `request.headers[\"x-request-id\"]`. `content-type` and `content-length` are included even though they arrive without the `HTTP_` prefix. Use bracket syntax for these, since the names contain dashes.\n\n`server` holds everything else — `QUERY_STRING`, `REMOTE_ADDR`, `SERVER_NAME` — under the original uppercase names. `method`, `route`, and `url` are lifted out of `server` into their own fields, so look for them there rather than in `server`.\n\n## Reading and writing\n\nThe snapshot is read-only except for `out`. Set `request.out.status`, `request.out.header`, `request.out.cookie`, or `request.out.buffer` to change the response. `print()` appends to the response body.\n\nA component receives the caller's `props` for the duration of the call. Bearer restores the previous value after the call. When nested components write back the same field, the outer component write-back wins.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/type/string/", "string", "Types", "String\n`string` stores text or raw bytes in Capy.\n\nUse string literals, variables, parameters, and return values directly. Use `length`, `find`, `contains`, `replace`, `lower`, `upper`, and `substr` for common text work.\n\nUse `split_utf8` when you must split UTF-8 text by characters.\n") { found = true }
    print("</section>")
    print("<section class=\"search-section search-handler\"><h3>Handlers</h3>")
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/cli/", "cli", "Handlers", "CLI\n`CLI` defines the local command entrypoint.\n\nThe handler has one parameter named `request` with type `dval`. Read command data from the request snapshot. Use `print` to write command output.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/component/", "component", "Handlers", "COMPONENT\n`COMPONENT` marks a function as a renderable fragment. Another unit reaches it with `component()` or `component_render()`; it is never routed to directly by a URL.\n\nThe handler takes one parameter, `request`, of type `dval`, and returns nothing. Everything it `print`s becomes the fragment's output.\n\n## Plain and named handlers\n\nA file with a single fragment defines `COMPONENT`. Callers name just the file:\n\n    component_render(\"lib/card.capy\")\n\nA file that holds several related fragments gives each one a name, and callers select it with a `:` suffix:\n\n    component_render(\"lib/widgets.capy:CARD\")\n\nA caller inside that same file can use the bare suffix form, `component_render(\":CARD\")`, which resolves to the current file.\n\nNames are matched exactly and are conventionally uppercase.\n\n## Reading input\n\nCaller-supplied props arrive as `request.props`. Read them through `string()`, `s64()`, and the other converters so a missing key has a defined value:\n\n    var title := string(request.props.title, {fallback: \"Untitled\"})\n\n`request.props` is swapped in for the duration of the call and restored when the handler returns, so a component never disturbs its caller's props, and nested components each read their own. The rest of `request` — query, form, headers, session — is the live request and is shared with the caller.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\nThe first time a unit is used in a request, its `ONCE` handler runs before the component handler. A component may render other components; each nested call resolves relative paths against its own file.\n\nA caller that names a component Bearer cannot resolve gets a `500` status and a banner element in place of the fragment. The handler in this file never runs. Callers that treat a fragment as optional should guard with `length(component_resolve(name)) > 0`.") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/init/", "init", "Handlers", "INIT\n`INIT` defines the unit materialization hook.\n\nBearer runs this hook before dispatch in a request workspace. The handler has one parameter named `request` with type `dval`.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/once/", "once", "Handlers", "ONCE\n`ONCE` defines the request-local unit hook.\n\nBearer runs this hook before the first unit entry or component call. The handler has one parameter named `request` with type `dval`.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/render/", "render", "Handlers", "RENDER\n`RENDER` is the HTTP entrypoint of a unit. When a request routes to a `.capy` file, Bearer calls that file's `RENDER` handler.\n\nThe handler takes one parameter, `request`, of type `dval`, and returns nothing. Everything it `print`s becomes the response body.\n\n## A minimal page\n\n    function RENDER(request : dval) {\n        print(\"<h1>Hello</h1>\")\n    }\n\nRead input from the request snapshot with a converter and a fallback, so a missing parameter has a defined value:\n\n    var name := string(request.query.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n\n## Response control\n\n`print()` writes the body. Set status, headers, and cookies in `request.out`:\n\n    request.out.status = 404\n    request.out.headers[\"Cache-Control\"] = \"no-store\"\n    request.out.cookies[\"visited\"] = \"1\"\n\n`redirect()` sets the status and `Location` header together. A handler may `return` early at any point; whatever it printed so far is still sent.\n\n## Named handlers\n\n`RENDER:NAME` declares an additional named entrypoint in the same file, selected by the caller rather than by the URL path. A file's unnamed `RENDER` remains the one that plain routing reaches.\n\n## What runs around it\n\nThe unit's `ONCE` handler runs before `RENDER` the first time the unit is used in a request. Fragments are pulled in with `component_render()` or `component()`; another unit's exported functions are reached with `unit_load()` or `unit_call()`.\n\nEvery Capy unit must export at least one handler — `RENDER`, `CLI`, `COMPONENT`, `WS`, `ONCE`, or `INIT` — or metadata via `#exports`. A file with none fails to compile.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/handler/ws/", "ws", "Handlers", "WS\n`WS` defines the WebSocket message handler.\n\nThe handler has one parameter named `request` with type `dval`. Read the message from `request.body`. Read connection data from `request.websocket`.\n\nUse `ws_send`, `ws_send_to`, or `ws_close` to affect the connection.\n") { found = true }
    print("</section>")
    print("<section class=\"search-section search-how-to\"><h3>How-to</h3>")
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/how-to/read-request-context/", "read-request-context", "How-to", "read-request-context\nHandlers receive request data in `request`.\n\nUse `request.url.script` for the script URL. Use `request.url.base` for the base URL. Use `request.route.path`, `request.route.page`, `request.route.raw_path`, and `request.route.valid` for route data.\n") { found = true }
    print("</section>")
    print("<section class=\"search-section search-guide\"><h3>Learn Capy</h3>")
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/install-and-first-program/", "1. Getting started", "Learn Capy", "1. Getting started\nCapy programs run as Bearer units. A source file uses the `.capy` suffix. Bearer compiles the file when a request first uses it.\n\n## Your first page\n\nCreate `site/hello.capy`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"Hello, Capy!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Capy!\n\n```\n\n`RENDER` is the entry point for a web request. `print` adds text to the response. The `\\n` escape adds a newline.\n\nRequest the page through Bearer's default local HTTP socket:\n\n```text\ncurl --unix-socket /run/bearer/http.sock http://localhost/hello.capy\n```\n\nThe command prints the response body. Bearer logs compiler and runtime errors.\n\n## Add a value\n\nA local declaration uses `:=`. Capy infers the type from the value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`print` accepts several arguments and writes them in order.\n\n## Write HTML\n\nA markup expression produces escaped HTML:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(<><h1>Hello, <?= name ?>!</h1></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`print` writes the markup value to the response. A markup expression alone does not produce output. The markup chapter explains escaping.\n\n## If it does not run\n\nConfirm that `bearer.service` is active. The source file must be below the configured site directory. The CLI helper must use the configured local socket.\n\n## Make the first change small\n\nChange one value and request the page again. For example, change `Ada` to your name. This proves that Bearer sees the file you edited.\n\nThen add one standard-library call, such as `request.out.headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\"`. Small changes make compiler errors easier to isolate.\n\nContinue with basic source syntax before you add request data or application APIs.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/source-structure-and-syntax/", "2. Basic syntax", "Learn Capy", "2. Basic syntax\nA Capy file contains declarations. Functions, handlers, structs, and type aliases are declarations. Code that performs work belongs inside a function or handler.\n\n## A complete source file\n\n```capy\n// A comment continues to the end of the line.\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var greeting := \"Hello\"\n    print(greeting, \", Capy!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Capy!\n\n```\n\nThis file has one comment and one handler. Names are case-sensitive, so `greeting` and `Greeting` are different names.\n\n## Functions\n\nA function declaration has a name, parameters, a result type, and a body:\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(add(20, 22), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe `->` expression supplies the function result. A function that returns no value has the result type `void`. Handler declarations omit a result type because handlers write a response instead of returning one.\n\n## Statements and expressions\n\nWhitespace separates tokens but does not end an expression. Commas separate arguments and collection items. Most lines do not need a semicolon.\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := add(\n        20,\n        22\n    )\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA block starts with `{` and ends with `}`. Its expressions run from top to bottom.\n\n## Comments\n\nUse `//` for a line comment:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    // Explain why the next operation is necessary.\n    var retries := 3\n    print(retries, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nComments do not change the program. Prefer names that make routine code clear without a comment.\n\n## Reserved names\n\nApplication code cannot declare host functions. Names that start with `__bearer_` are reserved for the standard library. Later chapters introduce the handler names that Bearer calls.\n\n## Keep the unit shape simple\n\nPut helper functions near the handler that uses them. Move a helper only when several units share it. This keeps the request flow easy to read.\n\nUse one top-level entry point for each route. Add named handlers only when the route needs separate component, WebSocket, task, or export entry points.\n\nA source file can declare types, structs, functions, and handlers in any order. Prefer the order that helps the next reader: data shapes first, helpers next, and handlers last.\n\n## Read syntax errors locally\n\nThe compiler reports the source path, line, and column. Look at that location first. If a block misses `-> expression`, the error points at the value-producing block.\n\nDo not use a newline to end an expression. Newlines are whitespace in normal code. Use braces and explicit block yields to show where a value comes from.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/values-and-types/", "3. Types", "Learn Capy", "3. Types\nEvery Capy value has a type. The compiler checks types before the program runs. It usually infers the type of a local value.\n\n## Integers\n\nAn integer literal has type `s32` when no other type is stated:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var answer := 42\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n42\n\n```\n\nState a wider type on the local, or construct the value explicitly:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var signed : s64 = 8\n    var inferred_signed := s64(9)\n    var unsigned : u64 = 10\n    var inferred_unsigned := u64(11)\n    print(signed, \" \", inferred_signed, \" \", unsigned, \" \", inferred_unsigned, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nBoth forms produce the stated wide integer type. `var signed : s64 = s64(8)` also compiles, but the constructor repeats information already supplied by the annotation.\n\nParameters, function results, assignments, typed arrays, struct fields, and the other operand in an expression can also state the integer type. Thus, a function that accepts `u64` can receive the plain literal `8`. An unconstrained literal remains `s32`.\n\nArithmetic requires operands of the same type. Use a constructor when the surrounding code does not state the required type.\n\n## Booleans and floating-point numbers\n\nA `bool` is either `true` or `false`. Conditions require a boolean value.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var enabled := true\n    var ratio := 2.5\n    print(enabled, \" \", ratio, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA decimal point or exponent makes an `f64` literal.\n\n## Strings\n\nA `string` stores bytes:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var empty := \"\"\n    print(name, empty, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn empty string is still a string value. It is not missing data.\n\n## Arrays, tuples, and structs\n\nAn array contains values of one type:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA tuple has a fixed number of positions, which can have different types:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := (3, 4)\n    print(point[0], \" \", point[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA struct gives names to fixed fields:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nArrays and struct instances are mutable reference values inside one workspace. Assignment shares their identity. Tuples remain immutable.\n\nThe collections chapter covers these types in detail.\n\n## Dynamic values and none\n\nA `dval` holds data whose shape is known only at run time. The `none` value represents missing dynamic data:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var missing := none\n    print(profile.name, \" \", missing?, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`none` is different from `\"\"`, `false`, an empty map, and an empty list. A `dval` is also a mutable reference value inside one workspace. The dynamic-values chapter explains mutation, safe reads, and conversions.\n\n## Type aliases\n\nA type alias gives another name to a type:\n\n```capy\ntype Count = s64\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total : Count = 12\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn alias does not create a new runtime type.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/expressions-and-control-flow/", "4. Expressions and control flow", "Learn Capy", "4. Expressions and control flow\nAn expression produces a value or performs an action. Capy evaluates listed expressions from left to right.\n\n## Calculate a value\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var price := 6\n    var quantity := 2\n    var total := price * quantity\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n12\n\n```\n\n`*` runs before `+` and `-`. Parentheses make the intended order explicit.\n\n## Declare and assign locals\n\n`:=` declares a local and infers its type. `=` replaces its value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var count := 1\n    count = count + 1\n    print(count, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA local exists until the end of its block. An inner block can declare a new local with the same name without changing the outer local.\n\n## Conditions\n\nUse `if` to select code:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    if total > 10 {\n        print(\"large\\n\")\n    } else {\n        print(\"small\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nCapy evaluates each condition as `bool(condition)`. A condition can use any value with a `bool` constructor. A string has no `bool` constructor and cannot be a condition.\n\nAn `if` with `else` can also produce a value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    var label := if total > 10 {\n        -> \"large\"\n    } else {\n        -> \"small\"\n    }\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nEach reachable branch that produces a value must produce the same type. A branch that returns or traps does not produce a value.\n\n## Loops\n\nA `while` loop repeats while its condition is true:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var index := 0\n    while index < 3 {\n        print(index, \"\\n\")\n        index = index + 1\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA `for` loop reads a half-open `s32` range, an array, or a `dval` map or list:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    for number := 1..4 {\n        print(number, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe range is half-open. `1..4` contains `1`, `2`, and `3`. A range permits one loop value only.\n\nOptional metadata follows the value:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(names : [string], profile : dval) {\n    for name, index := names { print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := profile { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show([\"Ada\"], {role: \"admin\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn array index has type `s32`. A `dval` key has type `string`. `dval` list keys are decimal strings. Structs are not iterable. The old `=` and `in` forms are invalid.\n\nArray and `dval` loops recheck the current count before each iteration. A loop can visit items added while it runs. Shrinkage cannot cause an invalid access. `dval` loop items are copies. An array loop retains its current managed item while the body runs.\n\nUse `break` to leave the nearest loop. Use `continue` to start its next iteration.\n\n## Blocks\n\nA block yields a value when its final reachable item starts with `->`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := {\n        var subtotal := 10\n        -> subtotal + 2\n    }\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis makes `total` equal to `12`. A block can keep temporary locals close to the calculation that uses them.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/functions-and-closures/", "5. Functions", "Learn Capy", "5. Functions\nA function gives a name to a calculation. Parameters describe its input. The declared result type describes its output.\n\n## Define and call a function\n\n```capy\nfunction greet(name : string) string {\n    -> \"Hello, \" + name\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(greet(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Ada\n\n```\n\nThe `->` expression supplies the function result. Use `return` when a function must finish early.\n\n## Parameters and defaults\n\nParameters have names and types:\n\n```capy\nfunction label(name : string, suffix : string = \"!\") string {\n    -> name + suffix\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(label(\"Capy\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`label(\"Capy\")` uses the default suffix. Defaults are trailing literals. A direct call can omit them, but a function value keeps the full parameter list.\n\nCapy evaluates arguments once from left to right.\n\n## Overloads\n\nSeveral functions can have the same name when their parameter types differ:\n\n```capy\nfunction describe(value : s32) string { -> \"integer\" }\nfunction describe(value : string) string { -> \"string\" }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(describe(42), \" \", describe(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe argument types select the overload. A result type never selects one.\n\n## Conversion parameters\n\nA parameter declared with `as` permits one constructor conversion:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(value : as string) {\n    print(value, \"\\n\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show(42)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe call behaves as if it passed `string(42)`. Capy does not chain several conversions.\n\n## Function values\n\nA function can be stored and called through a typed value:\n\n```capy\nfunction double(value : s32) s32 { -> value * 2 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var operation : function(value : s32) s32 = double\n    var answer := operation(21)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA lambda defines a small function at its point of use:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var add_one := function(value : s32) s32 {\n        -> value + 1\n    }\n    print(add_one(41), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA lambda can read captured outer values. Scalar captures copy. Array, `dval`, and struct captures share identity inside the workspace. A capture binding cannot be reassigned. The closure can mutate shared aggregate content.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var offset := 2\n    var values := [10]\n    var update := function() s32 {\n        values.push(20)\n        -> offset + length(values)\n    }\n    print(update(), \" \", values[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Store a callback in a dynamic value\n\nA `dval` can store a named function or a closure in the current workspace. Read it with its complete function type:\n\n```capy\nfunction add_one(value : s32) s32 { -> value + 1 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var step := 2\n    var callbacks := {named: add_one, captured: function(value : s32) s32 { -> value + step }}\n    var named : function(value : s32) s32 = callbacks.named\n    var captured : function(value : s32) s32 = callbacks.captured\n    print(named(1), \" \", captured(1), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n2 3\n\n```\n\n\nThe function type must match exactly. A mismatch traps. A callable does not cross a public boundary. BRRB, JSON, YAML, and XML replace it with `none`. Maps keep keys. Lists keep indexes.\n\n## Generic and variadic functions\n\nAn `any` parameter creates a compile-time generic function. Use it when the same operation is valid for several concrete types.\n\nA variadic parameter collects values into a typed array:\n\n```capy\nfunction print_all(...values : as string) {\n    for value := values {\n        print(value)\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print_all(\"one\", \" \", \"two\", \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `...items` to spread an array into a variadic call.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/strings-and-markup/", "6. Strings and HTML", "Learn Capy", "6. Strings and HTML\nUse strings for text and markup expressions for HTML. Markup escapes ordinary strings before they enter the response.\n\n## Escape dynamic text\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"<Ada>\"\n    print(<><p>Hello, <?= name ?></p></>, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n<p>Hello, &lt;Ada&gt;</p>\n\n```\n\n`<?= name ?>` selects an encoding from its location. HTML text and quoted attributes escape `&`, `<`, `>`, quotes, and apostrophes.\n\nInside a `script` element, a string becomes one self-contained JavaScript literal. The encoder protects the closing tag and JavaScript line separators. Inside a `style` element, a string becomes one self-contained CSS literal. Integer and Boolean values remain scalar values. `f64` interpolation is not supported in these elements.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n    print(<>\n        <script>const name = <?= name ?>;</script>\n        <style>.name::before { content: <?= name ?>; }</style>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nScript and style interpolation must start at a value boundary. Static source after it can apply an operator or a CSS unit. Do not put interpolation inside an existing string, template literal, or comment. Attribute values must use quotes. Tag and attribute names cannot contain interpolation.\n\nURL, event-handler, and inline-style attributes use HTML attribute escaping. Validate these values for their application context before interpolation.\n\n## String literals\n\nString literals use double quotes:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var message := \"Hello\"\n    var line := \"first\\nsecond\"\n    print(message, \" \", line, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe supported escapes include `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\\"`, and `\\\\`. String positions and lengths count bytes, not Unicode characters.\n\nUse `+` to join strings:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var greeting := \"Hello, \" + name\n    print(greeting, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse a constructor to convert a scalar when a function does not already accept `as string`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var label := \"Count: \" + string(3)\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Markup expressions\n\nA markup expression starts with `<>` and ends with `</>`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var title := \"Report\"\n    print(<>\n        <h1><?= title ?></h1>\n        <p>Ready</p>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe expression has type `markup`. It is a value. It does not write to the response by itself. Use `print(markup_value)` to output it. You can also return `markup` from an ordinary function or insert it into other markup.\n\n```capy\nfunction badge(label : string) markup {\n    -> <><strong><?= label ?></strong></>\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(badge(\"Ready\"))\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n<strong>Ready</strong>\n\n```\n\n\n## Interpolation rule\n\n`<?= value ?>` escapes output at the interpolation site. `<?: value ?>` emits output without escaping. It works only in HTML text.\n\nThe interpolation form selects escaping. The value type does not.\n\n## Common string operations\n\nUse `length`, `substr`, `find`, `contains`, `replace`, `lower`, `upper`, `split`, and `join` for ordinary text work. Use `html_escape` when an API needs escaped text instead of a `markup` value.\n\nStrings use automatic reference counting. Assignment and function calls preserve their lifetime. Most programs do not need to call `clone`.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/collections-and-records/", "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs", "Learn Capy", "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs\nUse an array for an ordered collection of one type. Use a tuple for a short fixed group. Use a struct when fields need names.\n\n## Arrays\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nAda\n\n```\n\nArray indexes start at zero. An invalid index traps.\n\nDeclare the item type when an array starts empty:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(\"Ada\")\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `clear`, `reserve`, and `resize` change an array.\n\n## Array values\n\nAn array is a mutable reference value inside one workspace:\n\n```capy\nfunction append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], \" \", second[2], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAssignment and parameter passing share the array identity. Returns and captures also share it. Content mutation through a parameter affects the caller. A parameter binding remains immutable, so `values = [3]` is invalid. Array growth preserves identity.\n\nA `for` loop reads array items in order:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe optional index follows the value and has type `s32`. The loop rechecks the current array length. It safely stops after shrinkage and can visit appended items. The loop retains each current managed item while the body runs.\n\nThe loop item is a value, not an assignable array position. Use an index to replace an item.\n\n## Tuples\n\nA tuple is immutable. It has fixed positions and can contain different types:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := (200, \"ok\")\n    print(result[0], \" \", result[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`(value)` only groups an expression. It does not create a one-item tuple.\n\nA tuple can spread into matching function parameters:\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -> left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Structs\n\nA struct defines a named fixed layout:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe generated constructor takes fields in declaration order. A struct instance is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity. Struct fields are assignable:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe field mutation affects the caller. The parameter binding itself remains immutable.\n\nA receiver-first function can use method syntax:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -> point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis is the same call as `total(point)`.\n\n## Choose the right shape\n\nUse an array when every item has one static type. Use a tuple for a small fixed return value. Use a struct for application data with a stable shape. Use a `dval` map or list for decoded input and data that crosses a Bearer boundary.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/dynamic-values/", "8. Dynamic values", "Learn Capy", "8. Dynamic values\nA `dval` holds data whose shape is known only at run time. JSON, YAML, request data, task props, and unit calls use `dval` values.\n\n## Read decoded data\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := json_decode(\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ada\\\"}\")\n    print(profile.name, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nAda\n\n```\n\n`json_decode` returns a `dval`. Member syntax reads a map key. Bracket syntax also works:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    print(profile[\"name\"], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse brackets for a dynamic key, a key that is not an identifier, or a list index.\n\n## Missing data\n\nA missing read returns `none` instead of changing the map or trapping:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var nickname := profile.nickname\n    if nickname? {\n        print(nickname)\n    } else {\n        print(\"guest\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`value?` is false only when the `dval` is `none`. An empty string, `false`, an empty map, and an empty list are present values.\n\nA key can exist and contain `none`. Use `has(profile, \"nickname\")` when that distinction matters.\n\n## Convert a scalar\n\nScalar constructors convert a `dval` to a static Capy type:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var name := string(profile.name)\n    var visits := s32(profile.visits)\n    print(name, \" \", visits, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe options map can set `fallback`. Capy uses it when the converted text is empty or cannot convert to the requested type. Omit the map when the type default is correct.\n\nUse `has(profile, \"name\")` before `get(profile, \"name\")` when a missing value is an error.\n\n## Create dynamic data\n\nA map literal creates a `dval` map:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var user := {name: \"Ada\", active: true}\n    var empty := {}\n    print(user.name, \" \", empty?, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA bare list is a typed array. Wrap it with `dval` when it must be a dynamic list. `length(value)` returns the number of entries in a `dval` map or list. It traps if the `dval` is a scalar:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var tags := dval([\"math\", \"logic\"])\n    print(tags[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Mutate a dval\n\nA `dval` is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity:\n\n```capy\nfunction set_email(profile : dval) {\n    profile.contact.email = \"ada@example.test\"\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {}\n    var alias := profile\n    set_email(alias)\n    print(profile.contact.email, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nContent mutation through the parameter affects the caller. The parameter binding remains immutable, so `profile = {}` is invalid.\n\nNested assignment mutates its target and returns the same identity. The root must be a named local, parameter, or captured `dval` binding. Temporary, call, member, array-item, and loop-item roots are invalid. Nested assignment creates missing maps. An existing list accepts only an in-range nonnegative index. Capy does not create sparse lists.\n\nThe `set`, `push`, `pop`, `delete`, `clear`, and `get_or_create` functions mutate their target. Each function returns that same identity. Reassignment remains valid:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {}\n    profile = set(profile, \"active\", true)\n    print(profile.active, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nMember and index reads return copied child values. Pure transformations return copies when their API specifies a copy.\n\n## Iterate a dval\n\nUse the value-first loop forms:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(values : dval) {\n    for item := values { print(item, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := values { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show({name: \"Ada\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe key has type `string`. `dval` list keys are decimal strings. Each item is a copied `dval`. The loop uses a live view and rechecks the current count. A loop that keeps growing its iterable can continue.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nA `dval` keeps identity only inside one workspace. Bearer, module, component, task, custom export, codec, request, and serialization boundaries copy BRRB. Identity never crosses a boundary. A `dval` can hold a named function or closure only in its current workspace. A typed read requires the exact function signature. A mismatch traps. Public BRRB, JSON, YAML, and XML replace callable entries with `none`. Maps keep keys. Lists keep indexes. JSON and YAML preserve `none` as `null`. XML writes `none` as empty text and cannot recover the distinction.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/web-handlers-and-requests/", "9. Handling a web request", "Learn Capy", "9. Handling a web request\nA Capy Bearer handler receives one copied request snapshot. Declare every handler with `request : dval`.\n\n## Read the request snapshot\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n    request.out.headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\"\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, guest!\n\n```\n\nThe snapshot contains `method`, `query`, `form`, `params`, `headers`, `cookies`, `body`, `files`, `route`, `url`, `server`, `session`, `props`, `config`, `call`, `connection`, `unit`, `websocket`, and `out`.\n\n`route` contains `path`, `page`, `raw_path`, and `valid`. `url` contains `script` and `base`. `session` contains `id`, `name`, and `values`. `websocket` contains `connection_id`, `scope`, `opcode`, `binary`, and current-scope `connections`.\n\nMissing members are `none`. Use normal conversions for scalar values. For example, use `string(request.form.email)`, `s32(request.websocket.opcode)`, and `bool(request.websocket.binary)`.\n\n## Form a response\n\nUse `print`, `request.out.status`, `request.out.headers`, `request.out.cookies`, and `redirect` to change the response. Use a redirect after a successful state change so the user can refresh the page safely.\n\nUse `ws_send`, `ws_send_to`, and `ws_close` for WebSocket effects. Use `request.websocket.connections` to select current-scope clients.\n\n## Handle a form\n\nA handler normally uses one route for both the form and the POST result. Read `request.method` first. Read submitted fields from `request.form`.\n\nThis example shows the complete control flow. A GET request prints the form. A POST request validates the email, stores it in the session, queues a cookie, and redirects. Use `print` for every markup value that the handler must send. The documentation renderer runs the GET path.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    request.out.headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"\n    var method := string(request.method, {fallback: \"GET\"})\n\n    if method == \"POST\" {\n        var email := string(request.form.email)\n        if email == \"\" {\n            request.out.status = 422\n            print(<><p>Email is required.</p></>)\n            return\n        }\n\n        var session := session_start(\"account\")\n        session = session_set(\"email\", email)\n        request.out.cookies[\"has_account\"] = \"1\"\n        redirect(\"/account\")\n        return\n    }\n\n    print(<>\n        <form method=\"post\">\n            <label>Email <input name=\"email\" type=\"email\" /></label>\n            <button>Save</button>\n        </form>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Read uploaded files\n\nUploaded files appear in `request.files`. Each entry is a copied `dval` with the metadata that Bearer accepted for the request. Treat the original filename as user input.\n\nUse the generated request page for the exact file fields. Validate the file count, size, and type before you store anything.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var files := request.files\n    print(\"files=\", length(files), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Sessions\n\nSession effects return a state map with `id`, `name`, and `values`. Keep the returned value when later code needs changed session data. The handler request snapshot does not change.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var session := session_start(\"account\")\n    session = session_set(\"theme\", \"dark\")\n    print(string(session.values.theme), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## CLI and WebSocket handlers\n\nUse `CLI` for a local command through Bearer's private CLI socket. Read command input from the copied request snapshot:\n\n```capy\nfunction CLI(request : dval) {\n    print(string(request.body))\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `WS` for each WebSocket message. Read the message from `request.body`. Read the connection ID, scope, opcode, binary flag, and current-scope connections from `request.websocket`:\n\n```capy\nfunction WS(request : dval) {\n    ws_send(string(request.body), false)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`RENDER`, named `RENDER`, `COMPONENT`, named `COMPONENT`, `CLI`, `WS`, `TASK`, named `TASK`, `INIT`, `ONCE`, and custom HTTP handlers use `request : dval`. Handlers do not return application values.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/units-components-and-exports/", "10. Units and components", "Learn Capy", "10. Units and components\nEach Capy source file is an independent unit. A unit can expose functions and type metadata to other units. It can also render named components.\n\n## Export a function\n\n`#exports` lists the functions and types that another unit can inspect. A callable `(dval) dval` function can also be called through a module handle:\n\n```capy\n#exports echo\n\nfunction echo(input : dval) dval {\n    -> input\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := echo({message: \"ready\"})\n    print(result.message, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nready\n\n```\n\nA module-callable function has the exact signature `(dval) dval`. Private helper functions do not appear in `#exports`.\n\n## Call another unit\n\nA separate service unit can expose a data function:\n\n```capy\n#exports version\n\nfunction version(input : dval) dval {\n    -> {name: \"Capy\"}\n}\n\n```\n\n\nLoad and call it from a neighboring page:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var service := unit_load(\"/doc/examples/call_target.capy\")\n    var version := service.default_input()\n    print(string(version), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`unit_load` returns a request-local `module` handle. Module member calls are dynamic. `service.default_input()` calls the exported function named `default_input` on that loaded unit. Module calls copy input and result `dval` values as BRRB. `unit_call(path, name, input)` performs the same operation without keeping a handle.\n\nRelative paths resolve from the calling unit. Use an absolute site path when the target must not depend on the caller directory.\n\n## Module handles\n\nA module handle is a verified request-local capability. It can pass through typed locals, parameters, and results during that request. It cannot enter a `dval`, aggregate, closure, condition, or serialized boundary.\n\n## Static type imports\n\nUse `#import \"path\" as name` when the compiler must see exported Capy types from another unit. For example, `#import \"/doc/examples/call_target.capy\" as example` makes `example.VersionInfo` valid in type positions.\n\nA static import reads type metadata at compile time. It does not create a runtime module handle. Use `unit_load()` when the target unit must be selected at runtime.\n\nDo not retain a module handle for another request. A later request can load a newer valid source generation.\n\n## Components\n\nA component writes reusable response content through output functions such as `print`. A named component uses a handler name:\n\n```capy\nfunction COMPONENT:CARD(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    print(<><article><?= string(props.title, {fallback: \"Untitled\"}) ?></article></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nRender it into the response:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    component_render(\"/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD\", {title: \"News\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `component()` when the caller needs the component output as a string. Bearer copies props into the component request context and restores the earlier props after the call.\n\n## Boundary rules\n\nBearer copies `dval` values at module, component, task, export, codec, request, and serialization boundaries. A target cannot mutate the caller's value through a boundary. The dynamic-values guide defines the identity rule.\n\nMissing units, missing exports, and incompatible exported signatures are errors.\n\n## Choose a boundary\n\nUse a component when the result is markup for the current page. Use an exported function when the result is data. Use a direct helper function only inside one source file.\n\nA module loaded with `unit_load()` lives only for the current request. Store data in a session, a file, a database, or a task result when it must outlive the request.\n\nKeep component props narrow. Pass only the values the component needs. This makes examples, tests, and future replacement easier.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/tasks-and-jobs/", "11. Tasks and jobs", "Learn Capy", "11. Tasks and jobs\nA task runs a Capy handler outside the current request. A job runs an operating-system command. Both return immediately with a handle that the caller can inspect later.\n\n## Read status data\n\nTask status is a `dval` because fields can vary with the state:\n\n```capy\nfunction state_label(status : dval) string {\n    -> string(status.state, {fallback: \"missing\"})\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(state_label({state: \"queued\"}), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nqueued\n\n```\n\nReal task states are `queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `canceled`, or `missing`.\n\n## Define a task handler\n\nA named task handler receives one request value:\n\n```capy\nfunction TASK:STORE(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    var key := string(props.key)\n    if key == \"\" {\n        trap()\n    }\n    // Store the application result under key.\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"task handler ready\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe task boundary copies props as BRRB. `dval` identity does not cross it. The worker does not receive the caller's request, session, connections, open handles, or memory state.\n\n## Submit a task\n\n`task` returns an opaque string ID after Bearer admits the task:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var id := task(\"/tasks/store.capy:STORE\", {key: \"report-42\"})\n    print(id, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe call traps if Bearer cannot admit the task. Use `task_status(id)` to poll. Use `task_await(id, timeout_ms)` to wait for a state:\n\n```capy\nfunction show_failure(id : string) {\n    var status := task_await(id, 5000)\n    if string(status.state) == \"failed\" {\n        print(string(status.failure_code, {fallback: \"unknown\"}))\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"failure helper ready\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA wait timeout does not cancel active work. `task_cancel(id)` requests cancellation, but a running task can finish first. Cancellation does not undo an external effect.\n\nA successful task means that its handler finished. Task status does not carry an application result. Store results in application-owned persistent storage.\n\n## Run a command\n\nThe two-argument `shell_exec` form starts a bounded background operating-system job:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var job := shell_exec(\"printf ready\", {background: true, timeout_ms: 1000})\n    var status := job_await(job, 3000)\n    print(string(status.state, {fallback: \"missing\"}), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `job_status`, `job_await`, `job_result`, and `job_cancel` with the returned handle. Always set a deliberate command timeout. Validate the final state before reading result data.\n\nThe shell interprets `cmd`. Do not build it from request data or other untrusted input.\n\n## Failure and retries\n\nBearer does not retry a failed task automatically. Submit a new task only when the application can repeat the operation safely. Use an application idempotency key for work that changes external state.\n\nDo not wait for long work in a page request unless the response requires it. Treat task IDs and job handles as opaque values.\n\n## Keep task input small\n\nPass a `dval` that contains only stable identifiers and simple options. Do not pass a full request snapshot to a task. Request data can contain cookies, headers, and uploaded-file metadata that the task does not need.\n\nUse a database row, file path, or application key when work needs large input. Validate that reference again inside the task. A task can run after the user request has ended.\n\nUse a visible status page for long work. It should show queued, running, done, failed, and canceled states in user terms.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/errors-testing-and-style/", "12. Errors and testing", "Learn Capy", "12. Errors and testing\nCapy reports type and syntax errors before a unit runs. Runtime failures include the original Capy source location.\n\n## Handle expected missing data\n\nUse a fallback when absence is valid:\n\n```capy\nfunction display_name(profile : dval) string {\n    -> string(profile.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(display_name({}), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nguest\n\n```\n\nThe fallback handles both `none` and failed string conversion. It does not hide a compiler error or an unrelated runtime trap.\n\n## Compiler errors\n\nThe compiler rejects invalid syntax, unknown names, and incompatible types. For example, this assignment is invalid:\n\n```capy-error\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var count := 1\n    count = \"one\"\n}\n```\n\n`count` has type `s32`. Assignment cannot change its type to `string`.\n\nRead the first diagnostic and its source location. Later messages can be consequences of the first error.\n\n## Runtime traps\n\nUse strict access when missing data violates the program contract:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {}\n    if !has(profile, \"name\") { trap() }\n    var name := get(profile, \"name\")\n    print(name)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis traps if `name` is missing or the access path is unusable. Other traps include division by zero, invalid typed-array indexes, failed host operations, and an explicit `trap()`.\n\nDo not use a fallback for data that the program requires. A clear trap at the boundary is safer than incorrect work with a fabricated value.\n\n## Test through Bearer\n\nRun one page through the local CLI socket:\n\n```text\nscripts/bearer-cli /path/to/page.capy\n```\n\nThe CLI helper exposes the response body and runtime errors. Use an HTTP client or a repository HTTP test helper when a test must verify status and headers. Include failure cases for missing input, invalid conversions, and boundary errors.\n\nThe repository test suites exercise the compiler, Wasm runtime, source maps, components, units, tasks, and documentation examples:\n\n```text\nscripts/test_capy_native.sh\nscripts/test_capy_phase1.sh\nscripts/test_capy_reference.sh\n```\n\nRun the focused test while editing. Run the broader gate before deployment.\n\n## Keep code direct\n\nPrefer a small typed helper over repeated dynamic conversion. Keep request parsing near the request boundary. Convert a `dval` to a static type once the required shape is known.\n\nUse one name for one thing. Remove wrappers that only rename another function. Keep comments for behavior that the code cannot make clear.\n\n## Find API details\n\nThe language guide explains how Capy code fits together. The API reference gives exact standard-library signatures and failure behavior. Use the generated Capy signature on each API page.\n\n## Read failures as contracts\n\nA compile error usually means the source does not match the type contract. Fix that error before you inspect later messages.\n\nA runtime trap usually means the input did not match the application contract. Test required data with `has()` before you call `get()`. Use constructors with fallbacks when absence is valid.\n\nWrite tests for both paths. A success test proves the expected shape works. A failure test proves bad input cannot silently continue.\n") { found = true }
    if consider(tokens, "/doc/guide/coming-from-react/", "13. Coming from React", "Learn Capy", "13. Coming from React\nIf you have written React, most of what you know transfers — the parts that change are where rendering happens and what a component is allowed to hold.\n\nCapy renders on the server. A unit is a `.capy` file, a page is a `function RENDER`, and the output is HTML written with `print`. There is no virtual DOM, no client bundle, and no file-system router unless you write one.\n\n## The mapping\n\n| React | Capy |\n| --- | --- |\n| a page component | `function RENDER(request : dval)` |\n| a reusable component | `function COMPONENT(request : dval)` |\n| several components in one file | `function COMPONENT:NAME(request : dval)` |\n| `props` | `request.props` |\n| `return <div>…</div>` | `print(…)` |\n| rendering a child | `component_render(\"card\")` or `component(\"card\")` |\n\nA handler returns nothing. Whatever it prints becomes the response, so the React habit of building a tree and returning it becomes writing output in order:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, {fallback: \"guest\"})\n    print(\"<main><h1>Hello, \", name, \"</h1>\")\n    print(\"<p>Rendered on the server.</p></main>\", \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n<main><h1>Hello, guest</h1><p>Rendered on the server.</p></main>\n\n```\n\n\n## Components take explicit props\n\nA React component receives props as an object and can render children. A Capy component receives `request.props` and has no children or slots — if a fragment needs inner content, pass it as a prop or call another component yourself.\n\n```capy\nfunction COMPONENT(request : dval) {\n    var title := string(request.props.title, {fallback: \"Untitled\"})\n    print(\"<article><h3>\", title, \"</h3></article>\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe caller decides how the result is used. `component_render()` writes the fragment straight into the response, which is the common case in a layout. `component()` returns it as a string when you need to wrap, measure, or conditionally emit it.\n\nProps are scoped to the call and restored when it returns, so a component cannot disturb its caller's props and nested components each read their own.\n\n## There is no client state\n\nThis is the biggest adjustment. There is no `useState`, no effect hook, and no re-render — a handler runs once per request and produces final HTML.\n\nWhat replaces them:\n\n- **per-request shared state** — `request.call`, readable by every handler in the request\n- **work that runs once before a unit's handlers** — `function ONCE(request : dval)`\n- **work that runs once when a worker loads a unit** — `function INIT(request : dval)`\n- **state that outlives the request** — a session, a file, a database, or a task\n\nA module handle from `unit_load()` lives only for the current request; do not stash one for later.\n\n## Escaping\n\nMarkup literals escape interpolated values for you, which is the equivalent of React escaping `{value}` by default:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var supplied := string(request.query.q, {fallback: \"<script>\"})\n    print(<><p><?= supplied ?></p></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `html_escape()` when you are building a string by concatenation instead.\n\n## What Bearer deliberately does not have\n\nNo asset registry, no island registry, no JSX-style component tags, no built-in children or slots. Routing is application code — the starter example checks page files and parent index handlers, which keeps the rules visible rather than implied by directory layout.\n\n## When something does not compile\n\nBearer reports the source path and the compiler output, and writes the full output under `BIN_DIRECTORY`. A compile error names the unit and line, not a bundler stack.\n") { found = true }
    print("</section>")
    if !found { print("<p class=\"no-results\">No results for \"<strong>", html_escape(query), "</strong>\"</p>") }
    print("</main>")
}
